{"uid":"9da919884e452a26","name":"test_the_inner_bound_is_clamped_to_the_jobs_remaining_wall_clock","fullName":"tests.test_run_bounded_timeout_marker#test_the_inner_bound_is_clamped_to_the_jobs_remaining_wall_clock","historyId":"80ee3ffea805ce1fa7a80eebfd66d647","time":{"start":1791969005624,"stop":1791969009651,"duration":4027},"description":"A declared inner bound is an upper bound, not a promise: if earlier\n    steps were slow, a step's own bound can outlive the job's\n    `timeout-minutes` and the runner's ambiguous job-level kill lands first.\n    With the job's start and bound exported, the wrapper clamps itself down\n    to the budget that is actually left, so the expiry (and its evidence)\n    happens from the inside, in time to be reported.\n\n    The clamped figure is read out of the wrapper's own line rather than\n    hardcoded, and then required to be consistent everywhere it appears.\n    The nominal budget here is 3s (60s of job bound minus a 57s tail), but\n    the exact integer is inherently either 3 or 2: this test can only export\n    whole seconds (`int(time.time())`, truncated DOWN), while the wrapper\n    reads its own `date +%s` a moment later, so whenever the process spawn\n    crosses a second boundary the wrapper correctly computes one second\n    less. Pinning the digit made this node flake on a loaded runner (gate\n    35066139757 at 7d74c00f: \"exceeded its internal bound of 2s\"); pinning\n    the *agreement* of all three reports of it, plus the clamp itself and\n    the from-the-inside expiry, is what the wrapper actually owes.","descriptionHtml":"<p>A declared inner bound is an upper bound, not a promise: if earlier<br />\nsteps were slow, a step's own bound can outlive the job's<br />\n<code>timeout-minutes</code> and the runner's ambiguous job-level kill lands first.<br />\nWith the job's start and bound exported, the wrapper clamps itself down<br />\nto the budget that is actually left, so the expiry (and its evidence)<br />\nhappens from the inside, in time to be reported.</p>\n<pre><code>The clamped figure is read out of the wrapper's own line rather than\nhardcoded, and then required to be consistent everywhere it appears.\nThe nominal budget here is 3s (60s of job bound minus a 57s tail), but\nthe exact integer is inherently either 3 or 2: this test can only export\nwhole seconds (`int(time.time())`, truncated DOWN), while the wrapper\nreads its own `date +%s` a moment later, so whenever the process spawn\ncrosses a second boundary the wrapper correctly computes one second\nless. Pinning the digit made this node flake on a loaded runner (gate\n35066139757 at 7d74c00f: &quot;exceeded its internal bound of 2s&quot;); pinning\nthe *agreement* of all three reports of it, plus the clamp itself and\nthe from-the-inside expiry, is what the wrapper actually owes.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791968776913,"stop":1791968776914,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791968776914,"stop":1791968776914,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"monkeypatch","time":{"start":1791969005602,"stop":1791969005602,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"_isolate_ambient_home_in_process","time":{"start":1791969005602,"stop":1791969005609,"duration":7},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCou